good evening the back and forth battle  between the church and state during this  pandemic has been pretty tough when it  comes to covid regulations but tonight  churches have won a decisive victory  thanks for joining us at 11 i'm denise  valdez  good evening i'm brian loftis a federal  court ruling to change the capacity  limits at houses of worship in nevada  up to 25 percent orco mana on this story  he joins us live with  what exactly this means zorko  well brian and denise services at  churches like this one have looked very  different because of the pandemic  many of them happening online this court  ruling  is good news for some congregations but  many tell us  they'll still be playing it safe we have  lost a lot of people to this  to this to to colburn kelsey west is the  senior pastor at nehemiah ministries his  church has the proper social distancing  and sanitation measures in place but  because coronavirus has directly  impacted his congregation he'll continue  to only do virtual services  we're still able to do the work of the  lord  and i don't have to physically put  people in harm's way  this comes even as churches in nevada  now have new covet 19 regulations a  federal court overturned an order  tuesday that upheld limited gatherings  saying houses of worship can have 25  percent of their capacity  instead of the 50 person limit put in  place by governor steve cesalac  cantor jessica hutchings with  congregation near tamid says they're  also  fully online they even stopped parking  lot services when the statewide pause  was announced last month  we have a committee of doctors and board  members that make decisions based on  what our government is advising and what  is  safe for our community so we will be  meeting to discuss  how and if this changes anything that  we're doing right now  church goers we spoke to say they'll  continue to stay home  i'm afraid of what would happen  i think it's better to do it virtually  but i just want to be on the safe side  so right now i don't go to church  basically while some look forward to  in-person services during the holidays  pastor west has one simple message  this season i just want people to be  safe that's my prayer  governor sysolak said in a statement  that he was disappointed  in the court's decision but he will  follow it he's also still  urging congregations and churches that  choose to hold  in-person services to be safe by  requiring masks and strict social  distancing  reporting live in las vegas tonight orco  mana 8 news now
 

